# DuetSoftwareFramework packages from a separately published build of our
# own, installed in place of the Duet3D archive's.
#
# One line per package:
#
#   <package> sha256:<checksum of the published .deb> <https location>
#
# The DSF packages pin each other to one exact version and check it again at
# runtime, so a build of our own is installed as a whole: every package that
# pkg/build.sh --packages=progs,dwc,meta produces in the fork, at a version
# <dsf.version>+<suffix> (3.7.0~rc.2+mp.1 for a modified 3.7.0~rc.2).
# duetwebcontrol is among them: the build packages our fork of Duet Web
# Control, Meltingplot/DuetWebControl, at a version of the same form, and the
# meta package pins it. duetsd and reprapfirmware come from the archive, where
# the build's meta package pins them; reprapfirmware at dsf.rrf_version, which
# the meta package has to accept. An empty list installs the archive's
# packages. Only a published build is taken, checked against the pin: no local
# path, no fallback, a mismatch stops the build. See "Installing a
# DuetSoftwareFramework build of our own" in the README.
#
# A board firmware file is not a package of the build; it goes through
# firmware.list.

# 3.7.1-rc.1+mp.1: upstream v3.7-dev at 3.7.1-rc.1, which since 3.7.0 no
# longer rejects the skip keyword outside G-code files and reads the layer count, the filament used,
# the generator and the print time from the headers of more slicers (the
# filters are in config.json, which the image puts over the printer's copy on
# every boot), with the later upstream fixes for M997 S2, plugins and SPI,
# recover-duet, a tool for root that writes the firmware back to a mainboard
# waiting in its USB bootloader, the M99 that closes a macro, which DCS
# completes itself when the firmware aborts the file because newer firmware no
# longer replies to it, M122, which runs unbuffered so that no message box or
# macro request overtakes its report, M409, which DCS answers from its own
# copy of the object model once that is fully updated, passing only R1, array
# lengths, indexed keys and keys its copy cannot resolve on to the firmware,
# unbuffered codes, which hold back only the codes on their own stack level,
# so that an M409 sent while a macro runs on the same channel no longer
# deadlocks it, and unexpected empty replies, which DCS reports now instead
# of dropping them; plus our fix for a DCS crash on a non-finite expression
# result, our fix that keeps the patches coming for a model collection the
# firmware replaces, such as a board's drivers after an M999, the plugin
# services started together with DCS, so they come back when DCS is stopped
# and started again, and our work on the SPI link to the mainboard: a single
# corrupted exchange no longer resets the connection or puts both sides out
# of phase, a transfer may take up to 10 retries (MaxSbcRetries in
# config.json), the link recovers when the firmware restarts while DCS runs,
# DCS stops after 60 s without a complete transfer so that systemd starts it
# afresh, and it activates the link capabilities the firmware offers in
# limits.sbcLinkCapabilities, as the mainboard build in firmware.list does
# since 3.7.0+1.mp.6 (phase guard, sequence guard, extended link with response
# codes that tolerate bit errors), and counts its link recoveries in M122.
# When only DCS got bad data from the firmware, it exchanges the data again,
# as the firmware does, instead of restarting the full transfer; in the ESD
# test of 2026-11-03 four of five link events were such a bit error.
# M112 completes only once the emergency stop has reached the firmware, and
# our fixes for M20, M30, M36, M39, M111, M260/M261 with V, M472, M505,
# M581.1, M606 and M929 are in. M98.1 S<n> starts a macro on another code
# channel (n as the index in inputs[]: 0 HTTP, 1 Telnet, 4 PanelDue, 8 SBC,
# 10 second UART) and returns once it is queued, so that a trigger macro need
# not hold the other triggers back during a blocking macro. It waits for a
# busy channel, without a limit by default, which holds the calling channel
# meanwhile, or up to W<ms>, and then returns with result 1 and a warning; up
# to 3.7.0+mp.15 it gave up after 500 ms, as W500 does now. Only DCS knows it,
# so it works from macro files and DSF clients, not from M581.1 U actions or
# PanelDue. After the firmware resets, DCS reads the
# firmware and IAP file names of the mainboard again, which M997 and
# `DuetControlServer -u` need to update it. Every journal line of DCS and DPS
# carries the syslog priority of its entry, so that a flood of retry warnings
# no longer pushes errors out of the journal, and a lost connection to the
# firmware is reported as an error. DCS tags every packet of a code channel
# with the epoch of that channel and starts a new one whenever it aborts the
# channel, as the mainboard build in firmware.list does since 3.7.0+1.mp.9, so
# that the reply of a code that ran into an abort no longer completes the next
# code of the channel but is shown as a message of its own, and it drops what
# the firmware wrote for an earlier connection. An emergency stop reaches the
# firmware also while the closal of a macro is pending, an abort that crosses
# a macro request or closal no longer blocks the channel or stops DCS, the
# codes of a trigger macro are no longer held back behind other channels, a
# delete request that names the root of the virtual SD card no longer deletes
# all of it, and a stop of DCS waits for a running firmware update. Plugins
# get only the commands and connection modes their manifest allows. A Python
# plugin is started as `bash -c 'exec "$0" "$@"'` with the interpreter, its
# script and its arguments as positional parameters, so bash no longer
# interprets them and the plugin policy still sees bash start Python;
# DuetPluginService refuses an executable outside the plugin's directory and
# stops, for systemd to start it again, when DCS closes its connection. From
# upstream come feed hold with the status held, which the mainboard build in
# firmware.list reports since 3.7.0+1.mp.11, and the end of a job, which
# clears its file name from the object model and is no longer reported as
# finished when the firmware aborted it.
# A code that fails in a macro aborts the macro before its next code runs, as
# in RRF; before, the codes the macro had read ahead still ran. A firmware
# reset requested together with an emergency stop completes the stop, a stop
# also reaches the firmware when the data before it cannot be processed, and
# the halted status after M112 is no longer replaced by the firmware's state
# from before the stop. M997 refuses a UF2 file without a firmware image,
# which would leave the board erased, and no longer stops the plugins and
# restarts DCS for an invalid file. From upstream come the state of every axis
# and extruder driver and the CAN interface of each expansion board in the
# object model.
# Since 3.7.1-rc.1+mp.1 a code that RRF passes on to DCS and that fails there
# or cannot be parsed no longer leaves the RRF channel busy, nor is its error
# sent twice, and the SBC link no longer stalls when a job file read hangs
# while RRF asks for a macro or a message box. Job rewinds and chained job
# files no longer stop the link, the job loop or DCS, M581.1 with SBC fields
# works from macros and job files, timeouts and the firmware clock stay right
# when the system time changes, files that RRF writes on the Pi fail on a full
# disk and are synced when closed, runonce.g runs after config.g, and DWS is
# started again when it ends. DCS no longer stops on an expression nested too
# deeply, a date that .NET cannot hold or an event log it cannot write, and
# evaluates floor(), ceil(), round(), pow(), datetime() and oversized values
# as RRF does. fileexists() looks for a relative name in the system directory,
# as RRF does; the printer configuration passes absolute names only. DCS holds
# its file capabilities as permitted only and raises them just to check the
# DSF services that connect to it, so it opens every file with the rights of
# dsf. The root plugin service runs commands from plugins.json only when the
# file belongs to root, which on this image it does not, so it refuses them;
# the image disarms those commands anyway and has no root plugins. Plugins
# without root start under a profile named dsf.<id> through aa-exec unless
# DisableAppArmor is set, which this image sets, as it ships its own policy.
# From upstream come the expression and IPC code limits, binary literals, the
# order of aborts in AbortAllFilesAsync, an M24 that only releases a feed hold
# and keeps a paused job paused, and full model updates that no longer return
# stale data.
# The packages are built with root as owner of every file.
# Duet Web Control comes from our fork, 3.7.1-rc.1+mp.1, the tag
# pkg/dwc-version in the fork pins: upstream 3.7.1-rc.1, which always shows
# the feed hold button in the movement panel, with the CHX 350 operator
# interface, which
# leaves machine actions such as preheating, homing and filament change to the
# flow macros of the printer configuration, reports a flow as completed only
# when it reaches its `echo ";; Completed"` line, which it keeps out of the
# notifications, forgets a flow answer once its box closes, finds the step of
# a message box by its title and mode, lets the operator close an S1 box, ticks
# only the steps that were shown, shows the camera while a step moves the
# axes, locks the interface while the machine is starting, moves the axes
# while a job is paused, sets the bed, chamber and nozzle temperatures from
# their readings in the header, shows and sets the fans, warns of a stalled
# fan on every page and starts no job while one stands, lists the jobs on a
# USB stick at the panel and copies or starts them, shows the licence notices
# of both computers and history and analysis from the QualityAssurance
# plugin. The release of that Duet Web Control also carries the interface's
# SBC backend, which plugins.list pins at the same version.
# Duet3D has not published 3.7.1-rc.1 yet, and the meta package still accepts
# the firmware of 3.7.0-rc.2 (pkg/firmware-version in the fork), which
# dsf.rrf_version installs, although the archive carries 3.7.0 by now. The
# boards of the CHX 350 run the builds in firmware.list either way; from the
# package the image only uses the IAP binary of the mainboard update, which is
# the same in 3.7.0-rc.1 and 3.7.0-rc.2. Earlier builds: mp.15 of 3.7.0 was
# upstream 3.7.0, gave up an M98.1 for a busy channel after 500 ms and carried
# Duet Web Control 3.7.0+mp.8, mp.14 of 3.7.0 had M98.n in place of M98.1 S,
# let a macro run the codes it had read ahead after one of them failed and
# carried Duet Web Control 3.7.0+mp.7, mp.13 of 3.7.0 had no channel epoch
# and gave plugins every command whatever their manifest said, mp.12 of 3.7.0
# lost those file names after most
# resets of the firmware and wrote every journal line at priority info, mp.11
# carried Duet Web Control 3.7.0+mp.6, which mp.10 did too, mp.10 did not know
# M98.n, mp.9 of 3.7.0
# restarted the full transfer when only
# DCS got bad data and carried Duet Web Control 3.7.0+mp.5, mp.8 of 3.7.0
# carried our own fix of that
# deadlock, which also held a code back behind unbuffered codes on deeper
# stack levels, and gave up a transfer after 3 retries, mp.7 held the codes of
# a running macro back behind such an M409, which waits for that macro, mp.6
# still passed M409 for most keys on to the firmware and carried Duet Web
# Control 3.7.0+mp.4, which mp.5 did too, mp.5 still cancelled that M99 and
# buffered M122, mp.4 also carried Duet Web Control 3.7.0+mp.3, mp.3 started
# the plugin services with DCS and carried Duet Web Control 3.7.0+mp.2, mp.2
# paired the meta package with the firmware of 3.7.0-rc.2, mp.1 accepted that
# of 3.7.0-rc.1, mp.9 of 3.7.0-rc.2 brought the root ownership, mp.8 the
# object model fix, packaged as the build user (1001), mp.7 carried Duet Web
# Control 3.7.0-rc.2+mp.6 and the object model changes of upstream, mp.6
# 3.7.0-rc.2+mp.5, mp.5 3.7.0-rc.2+mp.4, mp.4 3.7.0-rc.2+mp.3, mp.3
# 3.7.0-rc.2+mp.2, mp.9 of 3.7.0-rc.1 brought remote sensors on CAN expansion
# boards, mp.8 checked N line numbers in job and macro files, mp.7 RRF-style
# checksums on numbered lines, mp.6 kept the object model in step after an SPI
# transfer timeout.
